# AGENTS.md template (cross-review gate)

The sections below describe the cross-review gate's contract. Merge them
into your project's own `AGENTS.md` (if one exists), or copy the whole
template into a new `AGENTS.md`. Adjust the example paths and branch names to
match the consuming project.

**Where this content needs to live for each backend** (their
auto-discovery behaviors differ):

- **Codex CLI** auto-loads `AGENTS.md` from the working root + ancestors
  as authoritative session instructions. Copy/merge into `AGENTS.md`
  at your repo root for Codex implementer sessions to see this
  contract.
- **Claude Code CLI** does NOT auto-load `AGENTS.md`. It auto-loads
  `CLAUDE.md` (project root + walk-up from cwd) and the user-global
  `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. For Claude implementer sessions to see this
  contract, ALSO copy/merge these sections into your project
  `CLAUDE.md` (or rely on the user-global one if these conventions
  are already there) — the same set as `AGENTS.md`, under INSTALL.md's
  rule: every section except one naming a backend you do not install. The Claude review wrapper's trust boundary
  guards against malicious REVIEWED-TREE `CLAUDE.md` instructions
  specifically because that is the file Claude auto-loads.

If your project does not yet have either file: at minimum create
`AGENTS.md` (so Codex implementer sessions see the contract) and
optionally `CLAUDE.md` with the same contract sections (so Claude
implementer sessions also see it).

---

## Code unit-test policy — all modes

- Never write, add, expand, rewrite, maintain, restore, or run code unit tests.
  This applies to sessions, reviewers, and delegated agents. Renaming a unit
  test as a probe, check, experiment, diagnostic, or verification script does
  not create an exception.
- Remove existing code unit tests and unit-test-only assets through the
  applicable authorized repository workflow. Loading this policy alone
  authorizes no changes to any other repository.
- The ban applies only to code unit tests. Live testing, manual checks,
  ordinary program runs, and functional validation remain permitted and
  important. Source inspection, mandatory code review, and builds remain
  available. Preserve production input validation, error handling, runtime
  assertions, and safety checks.
- Reviewers must not request missing code unit tests or treat unit-test
  deletion as a defect. Flag newly authored or restored code unit tests as
  policy violations; do not misclassify permitted testing.
- This ban takes precedence over instructions requiring code unit tests. Only
  an explicit operator decision can change the ban.

No project mode or invariant surface waives the ban. Carry this section into
both AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md and into delegated task instructions.

A centrally attached project can loosen the ban only through reviewed
`unit-tests` records in `.crg-project/project.conf` (`docs/private-governance-v1.md`);
its reviews then apply the configured scope. Once that configuration has landed,
restate the same scope here, and only as far as the operator's global
instructions allow; never wider than the configuration.

## Resource efficiency — all modes

Quality and correctness take priority; cross-review remains mandatory.
Available time and token capacity are operating constraints. An explicit cost
or delay complaint triggers a corrective checkpoint before another expensive
attempt; an elapsed-time explanation alone is not resolution. Reuse existing
usage/review evidence, identify the waste-producing path, and change that path
within authorized scope before repeating the work. Preserve required reviews,
provider authorization, candidate identity, and safety checks. Never broaden
an informational question into unrequested implementation or verification.

Use one owner per operation, targeted context reads, existing usage evidence,
and contract-backed verification. Avoid duplicate polling, shadow reviews, and
rechecks of completed work. Record the removed work and measured before/after
effect; label unmeasured savings explicitly. Apply this discipline to delegated
workers and both implementer instruction files. No automatic model downgrade,
additional paid audit, or new unit tests follows from an efficiency finding.

Cover changed behavior and relevant dependencies completely. Use targeted
searches to resolve concrete risks before broad reads; unchanged-code access
is available context, not a requirement to audit every file. Reuse already-read
evidence and end investigation once required coverage and identified risk
questions are resolved. Preserve applicable integration and safety checks;
neither fewer reads nor a low finding count alone demonstrates review quality.

## Roles

This project uses a two-agent **cross-review** convergence loop. Both
Claude and Codex can act as IMPLEMENTER and REVIEWER. The
`REVIEW_BACKEND` environment variable names the preferred REVIEWER (not the
committer); the pre-commit hook starts the review with THAT backend. To
get cross-review, the committer sets `REVIEW_BACKEND` to the OTHER
backend. Each commit and each merge is gated by an adversarial review
pass. A separate periodic review-surface audit (documented at
`docs/audit-protocol.md`) keeps the prompt template, wrapper, and
AGENTS.md from drifting against the BLOCKER trend log.

**Routing** (set `REVIEW_BACKEND` to the BACKEND that should REVIEW first,
before `git commit` — there is NO default; bare `git commit` without
this variable set is rejected by the hook):
- `REVIEW_BACKEND=codex`: `scripts/codex/auto-review.ps1` runs first. Set this when Claude is the implementer (so Codex normally reviews Claude's work).
- `REVIEW_BACKEND=claude`: `scripts/claude/auto-review.ps1` runs first. Set this when Codex is the implementer (so Claude normally reviews Codex's work).
- `REVIEW_BACKEND=both`: two review legs run in series, and each leg must end with a passing effective verdict. Token exhaustion may substitute the other wrapper inside either leg. Defense-in-depth for high-stakes commits. **Short-circuits on first failed leg**: if the first leg cannot produce a passing verdict (the commit is already going to be rejected), the second leg is skipped to avoid burning another API call on a commit that cannot land anyway.
- **Token-exhaustion fallback** (operator fallback ruling 2026-08-22, clarified 2026-09-07): a wrapper exit 4 is POSITIVELY-IDENTIFIED token exhaustion (a durable `.exhaustion.json` sidecar is written; anything ambiguous stays exit 3, fail-closed, never fallback-eligible). The hook re-runs the SAME staged review ONCE on the other backend inside the same invocation. Cross-review is preferred; a fallback that lands on the author's own provider is an internally recorded SELF-review accepted over a stalled gate. Successful direct and substituted reviews have one provider-neutral caller-visible PASS shape; provider identity remains in the verdict headers and sidecars. No caller flag skips a provider or requests substitution, no fallback state survives the invocation, and an implementer session must never adapt later commands to provider availability. In both-mode the legs route independently and may finish on the same provider, including for commit-wrapper edits; each leg still requires a separate complete passing review of the same candidate. Automatic substitution requires neither a Claude verdict nor distinct effective providers; required pass counts and identity checks remain unchanged. A codex-to-claude route is refused up front when the staged change touches the configured consistency doc (the claude wrapper fails closed on any diff touching it).
- **Host network refusal:** Claude `ConnectionRefused` remains fail-closed exit 3, not provider exhaustion. Authorize external network access for the outer host gate invocation, then re-run the same command unchanged; never change reviewer selection in response.

- **Claude (implementer when committing; reviewer when `REVIEW_BACKEND=claude`)** — owns code changes, worktrees, branches, commits,
  and merges. When invoked as REVIEWER via `scripts/claude/auto-review.ps1`,
  requires Claude Code 2.1.248 or later and runs in restricted mode with
  built-in availability limited to `Read,Grep,Glob`, matching pre-approval,
  `dontAsk` for unmatched requests, strict MCP configuration, and an explicit
  deny list including `mcp__*`. Authenticates via OAuth/keychain from the user's active
  Claude Code subscription (no API-key billing by default). Defaults to
  the most capable Claude model + max reasoning effort + no budget cap.
  Restricted mode confines file tools to the fresh
  `%TEMP%/crg-claude-review-<random>/reviewroot` working/additional directory
  and ignores user/project/local settings files. The explicit
  `--setting-sources user` selection retains trusted host user-global
  instructions while excluding project/local instruction sources.
  Reviewed-tree `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/` content remain review data rather
  than session configuration.

- **Codex (implementer when committing; reviewer when `REVIEW_BACKEND=codex`)** — invoked only through the wrapper scripts
  in `scripts/codex/`. Produces a verdict file with `BLOCKER` / `QUALITY` /
  `NOTE` findings. The wrapper pre-computes all review evidence and a
  complete read-only snapshot of the reviewed source into a throwaway
  scratch directory under `%TEMP%` — outside any git repo. Codex runs
  there with sandbox + approval locked down via the wrapper's per-
  invocation `-s read-only` + `-c approval_policy="never"` flags. The
  flags are passed per-invocation intentionally: pinning the review
  lockdown in `~/.codex/config.toml` would neuter every interactive
  Codex session the user runs outside the review process. The review's
  lockdown belongs to the review, not to the user's global Codex
  defaults.

## Claude reviewer auth setup

**Default: OAuth/keychain from your active Claude Code subscription.**
Claude Code 2.1.248 or later is required. The wrapper invokes `claude -p`
with `--restricted` and WITHOUT `--bare`. If you're already
logged into Claude Code on this machine (which any interactive `claude`
session establishes), the review wrapper picks up your credentials via
keychain. Restricted mode supplies the filesystem and settings boundary;
the isolated review-root working directory adds defense in depth.

**Alternative: API-key auth (NOT recommended — significantly more
expensive than subscription).** Only use if you have a specific
reason: a separate API-billing account, a CI environment where
keychain isn't available, etc.

- **Option A — `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` env var.** Simplest API-key path,
  but exposes the key in the process environment of any child of
  the same shell:
  ```powershell
  $env:ANTHROPIC_API_KEY = '<your-key>'
  ```

- **Option B — `--settings` with `apiKeyHelper`.** Indirect; the
  settings JSON points at a command that prints the API key on
  stdout. The key lives in a more secure location (Windows
  Credential Manager, a file with locked ACL, a secret-store CLI):

  ```json
  {"apiKeyHelper":"powershell -NoProfile -Command \"Get-Content <path-to-key-file>\""}
  ```

  Point the wrapper at it via `-SettingsPath '<path>'` per-invocation,
  or persistently:
  ```powershell
  [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('CLAUDE_REVIEW_SETTINGS', '<path-to-settings.json>', 'User')
  ```

If none of the auth paths resolve, the wrapper detects the resulting
"Not logged in" output from `claude` and emits a specific diagnostic
pointing back at this section.

## Operational: committing under each backend

Cross-review depends on the committing agent declaring itself via the
`REVIEW_BACKEND` env var. The pre-commit hook REJECTS bare `git commit`
when `REVIEW_BACKEND` is unset (no implicit default; an unset value
cannot silently route a Codex-authored commit to Codex review). Two
convenience wrappers exist to make the declaration hard to forget;
both set the env var (saved/restored around the call so later commits
in the same shell are unaffected) and forward args to `git commit`.
Hook-bypass flags (`--no-verify`, `-n`, and clustered short options
like `-nF` / `-nm` that include `n`) are REJECTED before forwarding:
the wrappers' purpose is enforcing cross-review routing, so they
refuse to participate in a bypass path. Use `CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP=1`
(audited, see "Never bypass" below) for the documented skip case.
Before either wrapper calls `git commit`, it also requires
`core.hooksPath` to be unset and verifies the default per-clone
dispatcher plus its adjacent bootstrap-written digest attestation. A
missing, reparse-point, disarmed, foreign, or digest-mismatched
dispatcher refuses before the commit boundary; repair only after
investigating an unexpected digest mismatch.

**When Codex is the implementer**, use the Codex commit wrapper (which
selects the OTHER backend — Claude — as the reviewer):

```powershell
scripts/codex/commit.ps1 -m "task X: short summary"
scripts/codex/commit.ps1 -F .commit-msg.tmp
scripts/codex/commit.ps1 --amend
```

**When Claude is the implementer**, use the Claude commit wrapper
(which selects Codex as the reviewer):

```powershell
scripts/claude/commit.ps1 -m "task X: short summary"
scripts/claude/commit.ps1 -F .commit-msg.tmp
scripts/claude/commit.ps1 --amend
```

If a wrapper is forgotten and `git commit` is invoked directly, the
pre-commit hook REJECTS the commit with a diagnostic pointing back to
the wrappers (no implicit default; cross-review requires the
committer to declare which backend reviews). Default to the matching
commit wrapper for your session; the explicit fallback if a wrapper
is impossible is to inline the env var around a single commit with
try/finally restoration:

```powershell
$prev = $env:REVIEW_BACKEND
$env:REVIEW_BACKEND = 'claude'
try { git commit -m "..." } finally {
  if ($null -ne $prev) { $env:REVIEW_BACKEND = $prev }
  else { Remove-Item -LiteralPath Env:REVIEW_BACKEND -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
}
```

For high-stakes commits where you want BOTH reviewers' verdicts before
landing, set `REVIEW_BACKEND=both` inline using the same scoped
save/restore pattern.

## Convergence Loop

```
1. Implementation
   - Make changes.
   - Stage with `git add <files>`.
   - Before the FIRST gate commit of every round, run the author
     pre-commit self-sweep (see "Author Pre-Commit Self-Sweep" below):
     verify every symbol/path/count/behavior claim in the diff against
     the tree (counts per the count-kind distinction in item 2 below —
     a machine-derived output count is re-derived; an exact
     inventory/count in narrative prose is rewritten as magnitude or
     deleted, never "corrected") so round 1 starts from a defensible
     state.
   - Commit using YOUR backend's commit wrapper so the OTHER backend
     reviews (cross-review):
     - Codex implementer: `scripts/codex/commit.ps1 -m "..."`
     - Claude implementer: `scripts/claude/commit.ps1 -m "..."`
2. Pre-commit review (automatic, scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit)
   - Hook reads `REVIEW_BACKEND` env var (codex / claude / both;
     no default -- bare git commit without it is rejected) and invokes the matching wrapper(s) with
     `-Scope Staged`.
   - CLEAN (review-wrapper exit 0, no findings) → the gate continues through
     terminal staged-tree identity checks and Git's commit processing.
   - NOTE-only (review-wrapper exit 0, VERDICT: NON-BLOCKING, only NOTE
     findings) → the gate continues. NOTE entries are informational; read
     them but no fix is expected this cycle.
   - QUALITY-only (review-wrapper exit 0, VERDICT: NON-BLOCKING) → the gate
     continues (2026-07 severity contract: QUALITY is a non-blocking
     follow-up). The wrapper stages candidate-bound QUALITY rows privately;
     the hook emits normalized provider-neutral finding lines and promotes the
     rows (best-effort) to `logs/review-followups.md` once every selected
     logical review leg has a passing effective verdict and the
     pre-publication identity checks hold. A later terminal identity refusal
     can retain that candidate-review transaction; its rows do not prove that
     a commit landed. Whether to fix a cited QUALITY finding in the
     same round is a judgment call — fix it when that makes sense,
     otherwise let the follow-up index carry it forward; it is never
     simply ignored.
   - BLOCKED (review-wrapper exit 2) → commit ABORTED. Fix each BLOCKER as a
     class, record it in the remediation record (below), re-stage, retry.
   - A review-wrapper exit 0 permits gate continuation; it does not prove a
     commit landed. Only an overall commit-wrapper or direct `git commit`
     process exit 0 proves landing. A completion report pairs that process
     outcome with the provider-neutral gate PASS required by the Completion
     Evidence Contract.
3. (After N commits) Pre-merge review
   - From the base branch with a clean tree:
     scripts/shared/auto-merge.ps1 -Branch <branch> -Base <base-branch> -Backend <codex|claude>
   - -Backend is REQUIRED: it names the preferred provider that starts the
     merge review. Positively identified exhaustion can substitute the whole
     panel automatically within this invocation.
     Choose the provider that did NOT author the branch when possible.
   - Wrapper runs a branch-scope review, then ff-merges on exit 0.
   - Only BLOCKER (exit 2) aborts a merge (2026-07 severity contract).
     QUALITY findings no longer block: the wrappers exit 0 for a
     QUALITY-only verdict, each child wrapper stages its findings, and
     the merge gate promotes them (best-effort) to
     logs/review-followups.md after the ff-merge succeeds — so a
     QUALITY-bearing branch ff-merges and an aborted merge never
     pollutes the index.
4. Never bypass
   - Do not use `git commit --no-verify`.
   - Do not use bare `git merge` to the base branch. Always go through
     auto-merge.ps1.
   - `git rebase --continue` and `git revert` do NOT fire the pre-commit
     hook — only a direct `git commit` does. For rebased landings,
     `git checkout <sha> -- <files>` then a direct `git commit`.
     `git merge --ff-only` creates no commit, so it does not fire the
     pre-commit hook — but base-branch landings still require the merge
     gate via `auto-merge.ps1`. The sole exception is the identity-record-
     attested pre-redesign review-kernel promotion documented in INSTALL.md,
     when the old merge gate cannot admit its own replacement. That exception
     covers only the exact kernel commit; the completion branch still lands
     through `auto-merge.ps1`.
   - Do not weaken the hook script, the review prompt, the wrapper,
     or the Codex sandbox config to make the loop pass.
   - CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP=1 is NOT for ordinary review-infra edits. Staged
     changes to either backend's wrapper or the shared prompt template
     ARE reviewed by HEAD's trusted wrapper via the per-backend
     trusted-copy path. Staged changes to `scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit`
     are ALSO reviewed by HEAD's trusted hook: the per-clone
     dispatcher at .git/hooks/pre-commit (installed by bootstrap.ps1)
     captures the exact `HEAD:scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` blob in a
     shell-owned buffer, verifies its byte length and blob OID, and
     evaluates THAT buffer in a fixed `/bin/sh` subshell — not the
     working-tree candidate or an executable temp file — so the
     running gate is always HEAD's last-good logic.
     CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP=1 is an explicit, append-logged escape ONLY for
     three narrow cases: (1) the initial bootstrap when HEAD has no
     wrapper to source; (2) a fix to a HEAD wrapper that is itself
     broken/corrupting so running it during the commit is unsafe; (3) the
     identity-record-attested pre-redesign review-kernel promotion documented
     in INSTALL.md, when the old merge gate cannot admit its own replacement.
```

## Author Pre-Commit Self-Sweep

The dominant source of gate churn is probabilistic assertion — comments,
docs, commit-message bodies, and output strings written from what feels
likely instead of what the tree actually says. The bulk of QUALITY findings
the gate raises are mechanically self-checkable BEFORE the commit ever
reaches the reviewer: stale comments, references to nonexistent symbols,
count/inventory drift, and doc claims that lag the code. The gate's
thoroughness is non-negotiable; the author's job is to make round 1 start
from a defensible state so convergence needs fewer rounds.

**Run the mechanical pre-pass first.** `powershell scripts\shared\author-lint.ps1`
flags the script-detectable subset of these defect classes at ~0 token cost
before the gate spends a reviewer round on them: at ERROR tier, bare
`file.ext:NNN` line anchors (the symbol-anchor rule below), self-narration of a
pending change's own landing in `History.md`, local-only review-artifact paths
cited as proof, and exact inventory/count claims in `.md` prose; at ADVISORY
tier, dead repo-relative references, dangling milestone tags, exact-magnitude
transient counts, and machine-local wiring state (the inventory + magnitude
count-drift classes also run one tier softer over staged source-file comments).
It runs BOTH as this manual aid AND mechanically as the pre-commit
hook's fail-fast pre-pass (a verified copy — HEAD's in the tracked
shape, a manifest-verified snapshot in the local shape — before the AI review): on error-tier
findings author-lint EXITS 3, which the hook reads to ABORT the commit (the hook
itself then exits 1), and `CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP_AUTHOR_LINT=1` skips ONLY that
pre-pass (the AI review still runs) for a linter false positive.
It is NOT the authoritative review gate — the AI gate stays authoritative for
everything the linter does not encode. Clear every error-tier
finding and judge each advisory before the first commit; then do the manual
sweep below for the classes the linter cannot mechanically catch (behavior
claims, count re-derivation against the live list, category self-review). See
the `author-lint.ps1` Review Infrastructure row for the full check list.

**Mandatory before the FIRST gate commit of every round:**

1. **Symbol-claim verification.** For EVERY symbol, file path, flag, env
   var, or system name written or edited in comments, docs, commit-message
   bodies, or output strings: grep the actual tree and confirm it exists
   with the stated shape/role. No exceptions for "obviously correct" names —
   those are where the misses live.
2. **Count re-derivation vs magnitude (two disciplines by count KIND).** A
   machine-DERIVED count a script computes or prints — a fixture total, a
   file/pass count, an "N categories" output string, a generated-report body —
   is RE-DERIVED from the live list it describes, never carried forward from
   memory or a previous edit; for those, the correct number IS the content. But
   an exact INVENTORY/COUNT of repo/project state written as narrative PROSE (a
   comment or doc sentence: `there are <N> handlers`, an `<N>-task chain`,
   `covers <N> formats`) is a drift-prone defect class in ITSELF, independent of
   whether the number is currently right: do NOT "correct" it to a fresh number
   (that re-seeds the same drift) — rewrite it as magnitude, or delete the
   sentence when the count is its only content. A source-comment count that
   echoes an ADJACENT code literal (`// took 6 rounds` beside
   `const ROUNDS: usize = 6`) is exempt (the code is the lookup key). A count
   inside a dated ENTRY BODY of the root `History.md` log is exempt too — a
   static, append-only snapshot whose counts cannot drift once written (the
   DRIFT objection only; a count there that mis-states a LIVE contract the
   same diff changes stays a factual finding). Stable
   lookup handles (SHAs, task IDs, dates, versions, exit codes, symbol names)
   stay exact.
3. **Behavior-claim check.** Every "X does Y" claim in changed prose is
   traced to the code path that does Y at the CURRENT revision. Future or
   intended behavior is written conditionally ("will, once X lands"), never
   as current fact.
4. **Category self-review.** Walk the diff once against the reviewer's own
   category list in `scripts/codex/review-prompt-template.md` (the
   per-category enumeration the wrapper parses) as the author-adversary,
   before the reviewer does.
5. **Class sweep on every fix round.** When the gate cites one instance,
   grep the whole diff (and the package, when the class is package-wide)
   for the defect class and fix every instance plus the adjacent class —
   one comprehensive round, not N rounds of poking the cited line. (This is
   discipline 1 of "Handling Review Feedback" below, restated here because
   the preflight is when it first pays.)

The self-sweep is evidence work, not new reasoning: items 1, 2, and 5 are
greps or re-counts; items 3 and 4 are tracing each prose claim to its
current code path and walking the diff against the reviewer's categories.
It costs the author minutes; each skipped item is a potential full gate
round plus a fix cycle.

## Handling Review Feedback (Iteration Discipline)

The convergence loop above describes the mechanics. This section
describes the discipline expected of the committer when a review round
returns findings. The dominant cycle-waste pattern observed across many
iterations is: reviewer cites one line, committer fixes that one line
only, next round reviewer finds the same defect class at a different
line, repeat. Every additional round costs reviewer time and tokens for
a defect class that was already known. The expectation is one-round
convergence per defect class.

When a review round returns findings, apply these five disciplines
**before** re-staging and retrying:

**1. Defect-class sweep, not single-line fix.** For each cited finding,
identify the defect class in one phrase ("stale comment about removed
control flow", "unverified CLI flag shipped without `--help` check",
"missing diagnostic on miss branch", "PowerShell `+` outside parens"),
then grep the entire diff (and the package, when the class is
package-wide) for OTHER instances of the same defect class. Fix all
instances in the same retry commit. The CLAUDE.md global directive
"Handling review feedback" rule 1 mandates this; reactive single-line
fixing reliably converts a one-round fix into a multi-round chase.

**2. Cross-reference sweep after every substantive fix.** When changing
a concept's spec, contract, or shape, find every section that mentions
it (matrix entries, flowcharts, summaries, dependency lists, files-
likely-touched lists, titles, ACs, snippets, dependency graphs in both
directions) and update them consistently in one pass. Stale cross-
references are the most common DOC-VS-CODE-DRIFT class; sweeping them
during the fix round avoids surfacing them next round.

**3. Read cited line + 30 lines of context.** Smaller reads miss nearby
anti-references or claims that become contradictory after the edit.
Comments and contract docs frequently cluster around the cited line;
the same defect class often lives within the immediate neighborhood
and needs the same fix.

**4. Paired-file mirror audit.** When the cited file has a paired
mirror (e.g., `scripts/codex/auto-review.ps1` paired with `scripts/
claude/auto-review.ps1`), open the mirror at the corresponding
line region and check whether the defect exists there too. If it does,
fix both in the same commit. Symmetric-wrapper asymmetric drift is a
defect class that has historically required dedicated rounds to
catch — the mirror audit catches it during the original round.

**5. Verify primary sources before applying a fix.** When the reviewer
cites code or a CLI flag, read that code or run `--help` to confirm the
claim is real. Do not ship runtime-affecting changes (CLI flag, env
var, config key) without the single-command verification that would
have caught a typo or analogy-error. The CLAUDE.md "Ground claims in
evidence" + "Verify after acting" rules apply: a source-level claim alone
does not prove that the new invocation works.

**Pre-retry checklist (answer yes to all three before re-staging):**
- Did the class-sweep grep run across the diff (and package, if class
  is package-wide)?
- Did the paired-file mirror audit run for every cited file that has a
  known mirror?
- Did the verification command run for every runtime-affecting change
  (CLI flag with `--help`, env var with documented activation path,
  file claim with `Test-Path`)?

**Remediation record (enforced).** The commit gate keeps each blocked
staged review's BLOCKER findings and refuses the retry, without a review,
until `crg-remediation.txt` in the working tree's own Git directory (for
example `.git/crg-remediation.txt`) gives each one it is asked to cover a
block (those of the blocked rounds of the last 14 days, the newest 20
rounds, up to 256 newest first; more also require the approach line
below):

```
finding <digest> [<digest> ...]
invariant <the rule every instance broke>
sweep <where and how every instance was searched for>
sites <every place changed, or why none needed to change>
```

with one block per defect class (a class of more than 63 findings
continues on further `finding` lines), and after two blocked rounds a final
`approach <why the approach is right, or how it was re-derived>` line. The
refusal lists up to 100 digests still missing and counts the rest; the
round files it names hold every one. Write the record from the sweep
actually made: the next review is shown the prior findings and the record
and checks the class across the whole diff.

If any answer is "no" or "did not apply", state which one where the next
round will actually read it — see "Reviewer independence between rounds"
below for which channel carries that by scope. A `Staged` re-run receives
no commit-log evidence, so in this pre-commit loop the gap belongs in the
remediation record or the diff (a comment at the site, or the doc line
beside it); a commit message alone reaches the next committer but not the
next `Staged` review.

The reviewer prompt at `scripts/codex/review-prompt-template.md`
mirrors this discipline in its `## Review goal` completeness mandate
(enumerate EVERY finding in one pass, then re-scan for what the first
sweep missed). The reviewer is expected to pre-sweep within a single
review; the committer is expected to sweep across rounds. Both
disciplines are necessary because the reviewer sees only the prior rounds'
BLOCKER findings and the remediation record, not their verdicts, and the
committer cannot see the rest of the codebase from the reviewer's vantage.

## Project mode (PROJECT-MODE.md)

A repo MAY declare its lifecycle phase in a root `PROJECT-MODE.md`
(grammar and semantics: the `## Project mode` section of
`scripts/codex/review-prompt-template.md`; full doctrine:
`docs/project-mode-doctrine-v1.md` in the package). The declared mode
governs behavior-preservation duties, plus the code unit-test scope when a
reviewed project configuration names a prototype allowance (see the code
unit-test policy above). Code quality is mode-invariant, and the reviewer gate stays authoritative in
every mode. A campaign supplies lifecycle context and current priority;
it is never an allowlist for project work, and failure to advance it is
never a finding by itself.

Example declaration (a `prototype` repo mid-cutover):

```
project-mode: v1
mode: prototype
campaign: cut over the legacy wire parser to framed v2 messages
invariant-surfaces:
- save-file format v3 header contract
- public scripting API error codes
```

Review-context rules:

- **The preceding trusted declaration governs the current change.** A
  candidate declaration never selects the rules used to review itself.
  A declaration edit may land beside other work; the whole diff is
  reviewed under the prior declaration, or under `shipped` defaults
  when the prior declaration is verified absent or positively malformed.
  A malformed prior declaration warns and falls back to those defaults;
  an indeterminate repository read still fails closed.
- **Treat `PROJECT-MODE.md` as ordinary reviewed configuration.** No
  operator sanction, declaration-only commit, or activation sequence is
  required. Candidate grammar and semantics are reviewed as part of the
  diff. `CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP=1` retains the ordinary audited-skip behavior;
  project-mode edits have no special skip status.
- **Never use the campaign as a scope boundary.** Work outside the
  campaign remains admissible and is judged against the diff's own
  stated intent. Campaign text informs priority and named cutover
  expectations only.
- **Project mode changes reviewer treatment, never author permission.**
  Invariant surfaces identify production contracts, not test exceptions.
  Prefer build output and captured ordinary program operation; cite the
  date, command, observed output, and revision under the contract below.

## Completion Evidence Contract

Completion is **pasted, reproducible evidence**, not an assertion of belief.
Your project's convention doc defines the completion CRITERION (adversarial-review-gate
PASS — a verdict with no BLOCKER finding — plus a green workspace/build gate); this
section defines the ARTIFACTS a completion report must contain so the criterion is
verifiable rather than claimed. A report that says "checks pass / gate clean"
without the artifacts below is **not** a completion — the most expensive failure
mode is a false "done" that the next reader trusts.

A completion report MUST carry each item below — pasted verbatim, or (where
an item itself says so) pointing at content the reviewer can actually read
during the review:

1. **The gate PASS outcome.** Paste the trusted gate scripts' own printed outcome
   lines — these ARE the reproducible evidence (the centralized verdict files under
   `logs/<backend>/reviews/` are forensic records, and neither the hook nor
   `auto-merge.ps1` prints a stable per-file path on success, so do not quote a
   drifting filename). For a COMMIT: process exit 0 together with the
   provider-neutral `[pre-commit] adversarial review PASS` line. The line names
   the pinned candidate's review verdict and ends with what that review
   covers (the source diff only, not target execution or deployment
   readiness); the `review result: review-feedback: open review tasks in
   TASKS.md` line above it gives the open review-task counts, and any
   `review result: review-feedback WARN:` line names feedback work left
   undone (tasks not added, a recovery copy left) to act on. A PASS is
   never evidence that the target runs or is ready to deploy; a later terminal identity refusal
   still aborts commit authorization, so a PASS line from a nonzero run is not
   completion evidence. For a MERGE: the `[auto-merge] panel:` line, the
   `[auto-merge] panel verdict` line, the `PASS` line, and — as the
   ACTUAL post-merge success proof — the final `[auto-merge] OK - '<base>' is now at
   <sha>` line (the `ff-merge … -> '<base>'` line prints BEFORE `git merge --ff-only`
   runs and a later failure still exits non-zero, so the `OK - … is now at` line, not
   the pre-merge `ff-merge` line, confirms the merge actually landed).
   Successful artifact centralization is intentionally quiet so automatic
   provider substitution cannot be inferred from extra child lines; a
   centralization WARNING with a preserved run dir remains part of the evidence
   when present. Quote the outcome lines exactly.
2. **The mechanical pre-pass result**, when the change adds Markdown /
   planning-doc / doc content: the
   `OK (no error-tier findings)` summary from author-lint against the CHANGED docs. The dogfood must
   examine the actual changed content, NOT an empty index — the DEFAULT scope scans
   only the STAGED diff, so it is meaningful ONLY when captured pre-commit while the
   doc change is staged (after commit/merge nothing is staged and the default scope
   prints its no-staged-docs line while examining nothing, which is NOT proof). So
   either: (a) capture the default-scope `powershell scripts\shared\author-lint.ps1`
   output PRE-COMMIT while the docs are staged, or (b) run an explicit-scope pass
   that does not depend on the index — `powershell scripts\shared\author-lint.ps1
   -Paths <changed-doc-1> <changed-doc-2>` (or `-Tree` for the whole doc tree) —
   which works at any time. Advisories are author-judged, so clear or knowingly
   accept each. These are required pasted commands, not optional — the linter is the
   cheap, reproducible proof that the script-detectable error-tier classes are clear
   before the gate spends tokens on them. (See the Author Pre-Commit Self-Sweep above
   and the `author-lint.ps1` Review Infrastructure row.)
3. **The code gate**, for any source change: the verification evidence per the
   reviewer's evidence contract. EVERY cited run — build, ordinary exercise of
   the launched interface, or functional validation — carries, at minimum: the DATE, the
   COMMAND or script exercised, the OBSERVED OUTPUT (pasted, or pointing at
   content the reviewer can actually read during the review), and the REVISION
   the run was performed on, named as precisely as the channel allows (HEAD's
   SHA before staging, the branch tip, or the commit under review). A citation
   missing its metadata weighs nothing, and test files merely being added are
   zero-weight evidence that anything works — evidence is what EXECUTED, not what was
   authored.

Magnitude-not-measurement (below) still applies to the surrounding prose (record
"many rounds; converged" not an exact round count); the EXACT items above — the
gate's printed outcome lines (`PASS` / panel / panel-verdict / the post-merge
`OK - … is now at` confirmation), the merge SHA, and
every citation's item-3 metadata (the DATE, the COMMAND, the named REVISION,
and the OBSERVED OUTPUT — pasted or pointed at, whichever form item 3's rule
admits for it) — are stable lookup handles carried exactly; magnitude phrasing
never strips the metadata item 3 makes load-bearing. Honest escalation (a worker
status file's escalation record) with the evidence of what was tried beats a false
completion every time.

## Land in smaller gated units

The gate re-reviews the WHOLE diff every round, so a single large landing pays its
entire edge-case surface on each fix cycle: a large new file re-litigates every
branch each time one BLOCKER finding is fixed (only BLOCKER forces a re-review
round under the 2026-07 severity contract — QUALITY passes and is recorded
best-effort as a follow-up), and the chase compounds. Prefer
landing in smaller, independently-gated commits/branches:

- A new module lands its CORE first, then coherent extensions as follow-up
  commits/branches. Every unit retains production validation and normal
  review; no unit authors or carries code unit tests. Applicable functional
  testing follows the coherent unit scope.
- A wide doc/spec edit lands as focused commits per concern, not one sweeping diff.

Smaller units reduce the surface repeated after a failure, but each unit also
pays fixed capture, review, merge, and publication costs. Do not assume that
more commits reduce total cost. Group related corrections into a coherent,
independently reviewable, building increment and use observed operation costs
when choosing the boundary. Do not split solely for a smaller diff or combine
unrelated work merely to avoid required review.

## One owner per role (the plan is not a filing cabinet)

Every role below has exactly ONE owner. The failure is another owner's content
accumulating inside the plan, and it is invisible from inside: each individual
addition is justified, and the sum is a document nobody can act on. The plan
legitimately owns several roles — what it must not hold is a role owned
elsewhere.

| role | sole owner |
|---|---|
| purpose, current baseline, future roadmap, ONE active package, current decisions and directives | the plan |
| durable protocol / safety / architecture / configuration / product contracts | domain documents |
| evidence, alternatives, measurements, selected designs | research dossiers |
| ordinary-operation output | captured runtime artifacts and functional-validation records; never fabricated evidence |
| completed packages, commit identities, exact history | git |
| review pass lines and verdict evidence | the gate's own receipts under `logs/` |
| concise pointers only — NEVER startup context | a history index |

- **ONE active package at a time.** A closed package retained "for context" is a
  completion log inside a scheduling document. Its record is the git history and
  the gate receipts, both of which are exact where prose drifts.
- **A reset means REMOVAL.** Retaining the previous plan as a `<details>` block
  or a historical appendix, while its contracts stay binding, is not a reset —
  scheduling becomes non-authoritative while the obligations do not, which is
  worse than leaving it alone. `<details>` hides nothing from model context,
  search, lint, or a reviewer.
- **Never put validation output, gate receipts, or scripts in the plan.** A
  script in prose cannot be run and will not be maintained; a receipt in prose
  is a worse copy of a record the gate already keeps.
- **Watch for the deletion asymmetry.** Where appending requires no proof and
  deleting requires demonstrating that every clause was extracted or superseded,
  addition is always the locally safer act — so the document grows without
  anyone choosing to grow it. If your conventions carry a never-delete
  directive, it belongs to durable CONTRACTS, not to a scheduling document.
- **A worker should not need to read the whole plan.** Needing to is the
  symptom: it means binding contracts could be anywhere in it. A brief that
  names the outcome, the applicable invariants, the files in scope, and the
  verification commands replaces that reading, and is the thing to fix when a
  worker starts reading everything defensively.

## Landing-sanction authority

Some acts the gate refuses are not defects — they are decisions only the
operator may make. Each has its own environment sanction, and every one of them
is an OPERATOR act: an agent never sets them, never suggests setting them as a
way past a refusal, and reports the refusal instead.

| sanction | permits |
|---|---|
| `CROSS_REVIEW_ALLOW_GATE_UNTRACK=1` | removing a gate component from tracking (a shape transition) |
| `CROSS_REVIEW_ALLOW_SEAL=1` | `bootstrap.ps1 -SealManifest` only for an exact frozen-v1.7, frozen-v2.0, or current-v2.1 LOCAL recovery shape; the seal creates a replacement baseline that must be followed immediately by full `-Force` and renderer `-Check` |
| `CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP=1` | skipping the AI review for one commit; mandatorily recorded in the local `refs/crg-review-skips` immutable commit chain with the exact staged tree, and refused outright for a staged gate-component removal |

The pattern behind the list is the point: **a trust-changing sanction lives
OUTSIDE candidate-controlled text.** A commit message cannot grant itself a
skip, and a reviewed tree's own instruction files are non-authoritative — so
trust-changing authority arrives through the environment of the person running
the command, which no diff can forge. `PROJECT-MODE.md` is not in this class:
the preceding trusted copy is review context, and a candidate edit has no
authority over its own review.

Two consequences for a worker:

- **A refusal naming a sanction is a HAND-OFF, not an obstacle.** Report it with
  the exact command, and stop. Working around it — even when the block looks
  technically wrong — converts an operator decision into an agent one.
- **State what the act was for.** A sanctioned landing's commit message is the
  only durable record of WHY the operator was asked, and the audit reads it
  later. `CROSS_REVIEW_SKIP` writes its own local audit-ref entry; the other
  sanctions do not, so justification exists only where the author puts it.

## Round-boundary capture (`git write-tree`)

The gate reviews the INDEX, not the working tree, so "what was reviewed" is the
staged tree — and that identity is `git write-tree`, not a branch name, not a
file list, not "the changes I made".

Capture it at every round boundary — before staging a fix, and after:

```
git write-tree
```

- **Before**, so a round's starting point is recoverable. A fix that makes
  things worse is reverted to a tree, not reconstructed from memory of edits.
- **After**, so the verdict you receive is bound to an exact tree. Two rounds
  that produce the same tree OID received the same review, whatever the
  intervening edits looked like — which is how a "the reviewer changed its mind"
  claim gets settled rather than argued.

An unstaged edit is invisible to the tree and therefore to the review. A round
whose fix sits unstaged has not been reviewed at all, however carefully it was
written.

## The narrow path (scope that grows is BUNDLED, never absorbed)

A landing does the work it was scoped to do. When the gate surfaces something
outside that scope — a pre-existing defect the diff did not introduce, an
adjacent subsystem the finding points at, a mechanism that turns out to want
redesign — the correct action is to BUNDLE it for a later, separately-scoped
landing, not to absorb it into the one in flight.

This is not a preference about tidiness. Absorbing out-of-scope work is
NON-CONVERGENT by construction: each absorbed item enlarges the diff, a larger
diff draws more findings, and findings against newly-absorbed code arrive in
code written this round. The landing's round count stops tracking the quality of
the change and starts tracking the defect count of everything it absorbed, which
no amount of careful fixing bounds.

- **Bundle forward, in writing.** Record the item in `TASKS.md` at the main
  checkout's root — per-user work tracking, never committed, where the gate
  itself adds tasks for the QUALITY and NOTE findings of a passing ordinary
  (not paired) Codex or Claude review (one
  per severity and citation), QUALITY first because QUALITY is the immediate
  follow-up — or wherever the
  project already keeps work it has decided not to do yet. The entry carries
  what was found, where, why it was deferred, and the verdict artifact that
  names it. `logs/review-followups.md` is NOT that place: it is an append-only
  machine index, not a record of decisions. A deferral is a written hand-off,
  not a silent drop.
- **Deferring is a judgement about VALUE, and it is legitimate.** A finding can
  be technically real and still not worth a round: one whose enabling condition
  does not occur in this repository, one whose blast radius is a sentence in a
  generated report, one whose obvious fix introduces a worse defect on a common
  path. State the reason; do not pretend the finding is wrong.
- **Scope is decided by the DIFF, not by authorship, so SIZE the diff up front.**
  An added line enters review scope and receives normal severity classification
  wherever its content came from — a wholesale file ADOPTION therefore puts
  every line of that file into scope, and no commit-message prose narrows it,
  because commit-log content is evidence data rather than an instruction. That
  is a sizing decision to make BEFORE the landing: adopt in bounded pieces, or
  accept knowingly that the review surface is the whole adopted file rather than
  the change.
- **Escalating the pattern is itself scope growth.** Noticing that a chase is not
  converging is worth one sentence and a bundled item. Building an explanatory
  model of why, and handing that model back as a decision to make, spends the
  round without landing anything.

## Design before code on any failure or lifecycle mechanism

Before the FIRST fix inside a failure, recovery, ordering, or lifecycle
mechanism, derive the whole thing once: enumerate the states and the failure
modes, decide each transition's outcome against the invariants, and land that
derivation as one round.

The reason is author-side. A fix written before the state machine is enumerated
is written blind to the transitions nobody has decided yet, so each such fix is
a guess about a design that does not exist on paper — and the next guess is
made against a mechanism the previous guess already changed. Deriving once is
not extra work: it is the same work, done where it can be done coherently
instead of one un-anchored piece at a time. The threat model is usually stated
outright in the first finding raised against the mechanism, which makes the
derivation possible immediately rather than eventually.

Signals that the derivation was skipped, each of which means STOP fixing and
re-derive:
- A `BLOCKER:` lands in lines the previous round's fix added. The remediation
  approach is itself the defect — the change under review has become the
  patches rather than the feature.
- Two consecutive blocked rounds on one commit. Ask what END STATE the
  invariants demand and whether it can be specified in one pass, rather than
  fixing a third finding.

Sweep at the SEMANTIC level, not the syntactic one: the invariant every finding
violates, not merely the API every finding cites.

## Reviewer independence between rounds

The reviewer cannot see the previous round's verdict, and that is deliberate —
each round is an independent adversarial read, which is what stops a reviewer
from ratifying its own earlier judgement. The one exception is a retry of a
blocked `Staged` change: the commit gate shows that review the earlier
rounds' BLOCKER findings and the author's remediation record, never the
verdicts, so it can check each class across the whole diff.

Consequences for the author:
- **A finding's absence in round N+1 is not agreement.** A different pass may
  simply have looked elsewhere. Do not treat silence as a retraction, and do not
  re-open a settled question because a later round did not repeat it.
- **Everything the next round needs must be in the artifacts, and WHICH artifact
  depends on the scope.** State what was fixed and what was deliberately NOT
  fixed, with the reason — an undocumented deliberate omission reads to the next
  pass as an oversight and returns as a finding. The carrying channel differs:
  `Commit` and `Branch` scope reviews receive the commit-log evidence channel,
  so a commit message reaches the next round there; `Staged` and `Uncommitted`
  scope reviews receive an EMPTY commit-log channel, so anything the next round
  must know has to live in the diff itself — a code comment or a doc line —
  except that a retry of a blocked `Staged` change also receives the
  remediation record's blocks for its prior findings, which can say how each
  class was swept and why a site was left unchanged. Writing it only in a
  commit message that a `Staged` review never sees is the same as not writing
  it.

## Fresh dispatch over deep resume

A session that has been iterating on one landing for many rounds carries the
accumulated framing of every round it has already argued. That framing is what
makes a chase feel like progress. Prefer dispatching a FRESH worker with a
written brief over resuming a deep session, whenever the work has a clean
boundary.

**Hand-off brief checklist** — a brief that omits any of these produces a worker
that re-derives it wrongly:
- The ONE outcome this landing must reach, and its explicit non-goals.
- The invariants that apply, named — not "follow the conventions".
- The files in scope, and the files deliberately out of scope.
- The verification commands that must pass, verbatim.
- What has already been deferred, so it is not re-absorbed.
- The current verdict artifact, if a round is in flight.

## Narrative-record discipline (symbol anchors, magnitude, frozen-snapshot marker)

These three rules keep tracked prose (commit messages, docs, this file) from
drifting as the tree moves; the reviewer enforces them under DOC-VS-CODE-DRIFT and
`author-lint.ps1` flags the mechanically-detectable subset.

- **Symbol anchors, not line numbers.** Source references in tracked docs and
  comments name the **symbol** (function / type / system / variant name), never a
  bare `file.ext:NNN` line number. Line numbers drift on every edit, so a
  line-anchored citation goes stale the moment any change touches the file and
  re-generates perpetual review nitpicks. Add a short locating phrase only if the
  symbol alone is ambiguous. Exception: a doc carrying the explicit
  frozen-snapshot marker (see below) keeps its line numbers as historical artifacts.
- **Magnitude, not measurement.** In narrative records (history logs, reports, doc
  prose) a transient quantity — iteration/round counts, attempt numbers, durations,
  assertion totals, line numbers — is written as magnitude and implication ("many
  rounds; high churn"), NOT an exact value, unless the exact number changes what a
  future reader does. An exact INVENTORY/COUNT of repo or project state stated in
  prose (`there are <N> handlers`, an `<N>-task chain`, `covers <N> formats`) is the
  same defect class in a durable form: the exact-count-in-prose is itself the defect
  regardless of whether the number is currently right, because it drifts the instant
  the inventory changes. The fix is magnitude phrasing or deleting the sentence when
  the count is its only content — NEVER a "corrected" number, which just re-seeds the
  drift. Stable lookup handles (commit SHAs, task IDs, dates, versions, exit codes,
  symbol names, dated artifact filenames) stay exact — those are references, not
  measurements; machine-DERIVED counts a script computes or prints (fixture totals,
  an "N categories" output string, a generated-report body) are re-derived from
  source, not carried as prose; and a source-comment count echoing an ADJACENT code
  literal (`// took 6 rounds` beside `const ROUNDS: usize = 6`) is exempt. False
  precision is drift bait: stale the moment the tree moves, and costlier to re-verify
  than it is worth. Also: never cite a local-only/untracked path as *verification
  evidence* for a claim in a tracked doc — a reference a fresh clone cannot resolve
  is dead on arrival (operational machine-local path targets named as patch/config
  instructions are not evidence citations).
- **Frozen-snapshot marker exemption.** A doc declares itself a frozen historical
  record with an explicit MARKER — the HTML comment `<!-- frozen-snapshot -->` on
  its own line near the top (invisible in rendered Markdown). The exemption is
  SCOPED to the SYMBOL-ANCHOR rule: when present, the doc's line-number citations
  are artifacts of when it ran and are left as-is, and `author-lint.ps1` downgrades
  its LINE-ANCHOR findings to advisory. The marker does NOT change MAGNITUDE
  handling — `author-lint.ps1` still runs the MAGNITUDE check on a marked doc, but
  author-lint's MAGNITUDE class is advisory-tier for every doc regardless (author-
  lint never gates on it). That is SEPARATE from the review gate: the review
  prompt's DOC-VS-CODE-DRIFT magnitude rule still classifies exact transient counts
  as a QUALITY finding (non-blocking under the 2026-07 severity contract, but
  recorded to the follow-up index; counts inside dated entry bodies of the root
  `History.md` log are exempt — the drift-only History exemption), and the marker
  does NOT exempt a doc from it. What the
  marker DOES exempt — at BOTH author-lint (LINE-ANCHOR → advisory) and the review
  gate (the DOC-VS-CODE-DRIFT frozen-line-number exemption) — is the doc's frozen
  LINE NUMBERS, not its transient counts. Detection: a
  line matching `^\s*<!--\s*frozen-snapshot\s*-->\s*$` (case-insensitive on the
  token) within the first ~30 lines, in normal Markdown context only — a marker
  inside a fenced code block or on a CommonMark indented-code line (>=4 leading
  spaces, or 0-3 spaces then a tab) renders as visible code and does NOT grant the
  exemption. The MARKER — not the filename or date — grants the
  exemption: a dated-in-name doc WITHOUT the marker keeps its LINE-ANCHORs
  error-tier (it may be live authority, or a hybrid whose sections are current —
  keep it unmarked and symbol-anchor those sections), and a non-dated doc WITH the
  marker has its line anchors downgraded.

## Review Infrastructure

Shared utility implementations live under `scripts/shared/`. The legacy
`scripts/codex/auto-merge.ps1` path remains a forwarding entrypoint for installed
launcher compatibility; new commands use `scripts/shared/auto-merge.ps1`.
The forwarding entrypoint preserves the shared gate's parameters and exit status.

The installed gate requires Git 2.41 or later. `bootstrap.ps1` behaviorally
probes the required attribute-source forms and refuses before target mutation
when either capability is unavailable.

Every reference below to repository/object/ref/index selectors includes
`GIT_REFERENCE_BACKEND`; Git 2.55 and later can use that variable to redirect
the ref database independently of the worktree path.

For `scripts/run-gate.sh`, the backend argument selects the preferred initial
reviewer; positively identified exhaustion may still trigger the gate's
automatic alternate-provider path. Before any Git child, the entry point
reduces every raw case variant of `GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS` to exactly one
canonical `GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1`, rejects prohibited selector names, and
removes every case variant of the retired
`CROSS_REVIEW_ALLOW_MODE_CHANGE` variable. The staged-set probe preserves
Git's producer status separately from strict NUL-framed pathname output;
producer failure, an empty record, or an unterminated record refuses, while a
well-formed zero-record result remains the ordinary empty-stage refusal.

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `bootstrap.ps1` | Installer for the cross-review gate. Validates root `CROSS-REVIEW-VALUES.md`, renders the prompt and installed `docs/AGENTS.md.template` inline before destination mutation, and copies the complete gate inventory. The values source remains project-owned and outside package payload, manifest, and LOCAL authorization. Appends the root `logs/` ignore rule (routed to the git common dir's `info/exclude` instead when the gate hook path is already ignored — the local shape's project-content protection), reconciles legacy `core.hooksPath`, installs the per-clone `.git/hooks/pre-commit` dispatcher, and records the P9-delivered install manifest plus package-version record (per-file SHA-256 and package version in the per-worktree git dir — each linked worktree seals its own baseline). Copied installs receive package/source provenance stamps; a self-install deliberately preserves the package's tracked source bytes unstamped while still publishing both records. The manifest anchors the LOCAL install shape (`docs/local-install-v1.md`: gate files untracked and deliberately ignored verify against the manifest instead of HEAD, admitted only alongside the operator-custody authorization marker that bootstrap records; the marker lists exactly the components observed untracked and ignored and is epoch-bound to the manifest) and the refresh loss-prevention (every pre-existing gate file is renamed to a sibling custody name, a hash-verified copy lands under `<git-common-dir>/crg-backups/`, and custody is archived only after the replacement itself verifies, with tracked paths graded against trusted HEAD first and the manifest consulted only after positive HEAD absence; every replaced upgrade path is reported, while a same-version refresh reports baseline and late-copy divergence; the per-clone dispatcher is the exception — it is rewritten IN PLACE through one exclusive handle so the live hook path never empties, with its recovery copies verified through held handles). `-S