## Hook Installation

A fresh clone needs the per-clone dispatcher installed at
`.git/hooks/pre-commit`. The dispatcher lives outside the working
tree (per-clone, not versioned) so a commit cannot edit or remove
it through the review flow — closing the `git rm scripts/git-hooks/
pre-commit` bypass that a versioned `core.hooksPath` install would
leave open.

Install via the package's bootstrap script:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <path-to>/cross-review-gate/bootstrap.ps1 -TargetRepo .
```

The bootstrap copies the in-tree gate files, appends `logs/` to the
target's root `.gitignore` (idempotent) so the gate's generated
artifacts stay untracked, and installs the dispatcher. It also
RECONCILES a prior `core.hooksPath`: the legacy `scripts/git-hooks`
value is auto-unset (superseded by the per-clone dispatcher), but any
OTHER set value — a custom hooks dir, or an empty `core.hooksPath=` —
ABORTS the install without `-Force` (with `-Force` it warns and leaves
the value set, so the dispatcher stays inert until you resolve it); it
also fails closed on a `git config` error or a masking `GIT_CONFIG_*`
env var. UNSET is the fully-supported state: the commit wrappers
likewise REQUIRE `core.hooksPath` unset, then require the ordinary default
dispatcher and adjacent bootstrap digest attestation to verify before
`git commit`; configured, missing, foreign, or tampered states abort. See
the package's `INSTALL.md` for the full setup (including the required
bootstrap-commit step that gets the gate files into HEAD).

Do NOT use `git config core.hooksPath scripts/git-hooks` directly:
that points git at a versioned-in-tree hook, which means a `git rm
scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` commit removes the executable before
git can invoke it — bypassing the gate with no audit trail. The
dispatcher closes this hole by living per-clone outside the
working tree.

## Verdict Severity Contract

Findings come back with one of three prefixes (parsed by the wrapper):

- `BLOCKER:` — real bug, regression, integration-boundary mismatch,
  dropped error path, silent failure, convention violation, or
  unmarked stub. Aborts the gate as exit 2 ONLY when a `VERDICT:` line
  is PRESENT (even an inconsistent one like `VERDICT: CLEAN` — a present
  BLOCKER prefix wins over a wrong verdict word). A BLOCKER finding with
  a MISSING or duplicate `VERDICT:` line is malformed output and fails
  closed as exit 3, not exit 2 — a verdict with no trustworthy
  `VERDICT:` line is never accepted as a blocker signal.
- `QUALITY:` — a NON-BLOCKING follow-up (2026-07 severity contract). A
  smaller real defect, OR a factually-wrong claim about the tree as it
  is today. Does NOT abort the gate: a QUALITY-only verdict exits 0
  (with `VERDICT: NON-BLOCKING`), the wrapper prints the findings
  prominently and stages them for the gate's post-pass best-effort
  promotion to logs/review-followups.md, for a follow-up session to
  fix as classes in one pass. Fixing a cited QUALITY finding in the
  same round is a judgment call; the finding is recorded and batched,
  never simply ignored.
- `NOTE:` — informational observation. Does NOT gate either
  pre-commit or merge.

`PLAN-CONSISTENCY.txt` has one severity contract. The canonical envelope starts
at byte-zero with exactly one `PLAN-CONSISTENCY-STATE: v1 <state>` line, where
the state is `produced`, `skipped-not-configured`, `skipped-not-in-diff`, or
`indeterminate`, and ends with `PLAN-CONSISTENCY-END: v1` as the final line. A
`PLAN-CONSISTENCY-FINDING:` row in a well-formed `produced` envelope is
documentary drift: `QUALITY:` under `PLAN-DRIFT`. Either valid skip state is a
non-finding. When the configured document is in the reviewed diff, an
`indeterminate` state or absent, unreadable, malformed, or unterminated
evidence is a fail-closed `BLOCKER:` under `SILENT-FAILURE`.
Independent planning defects that satisfy another named BLOCKER rule remain
BLOCKERs; the report mapping never downgrades them.

### QUALITY capture lifecycle

The gate's half is capture: it appends passing runs' `QUALITY:` findings to
`logs/review-followups.md`, grouped per run and carrying the verdict filename
that produced them. It removes nothing. It tags a row ONLY when the reviewer
claimed capture — see "SCOPED capture" below — and never triages. Everything
after capture is a duty of whoever works the index, and without that duty the
index only grows: an append-only file nobody clears stops being a work queue
and becomes a second, unread copy of the verdict logs.

- **DO NOT HAND-EDIT THE INDEX.** Entries are grouped into run-level
  transactions, each closed by a
  `<!-- review-followups: transaction v1 rows=<n> sha256=<hex> -->` record whose
  digest covers that transaction's header and every one of its rows. Deleting a
  single entry, or annotating one, invalidates that digest: validation then
  reads the transaction as incomplete, which disables review-log pruning and
  causes later appends to be dropped. A tidy-looking edit therefore breaks
  capture for every run after it.
- **Triage by COPYING OUT, not by editing in place.** Read the index, and turn
  each finding into work where the project actually schedules it — a task, an
  issue, a debt-register line — carrying the path, the line, and the verdict
  filename the entry names. That leaves the index byte-intact and puts the
  finding somewhere with an owner.
- **Tracked or dismissed, and recorded either way.** A finding either becomes
  scheduled work or is judged not worth doing; both outcomes are written down
  outside the index, with the reason for a dismissal. A finding that is neither
  is one nobody has read.
- **Clearing the index is a maintenance operation with a deadline, and it needs
  supported tooling.** Verdict directories auto-prune when an operator
  configures `CROSS_REVIEW_PRUNE_TOOL` (unset means nothing is deleted), but that pruning is
  quarantined while the index carries an unsealed legacy region — so an index
  left unattended eventually holds verdict retention hostage. There is no
  per-entry maintenance command today: the seal mode
  (`auto-review.ps1 -Scope SealLegacy`) is what exists, and a
  transaction-preserving prune command is future work rather than something to
  improvise by hand.
- **The index is execution state, not a record.** It is gitignored. Anything that
  must survive a clone belongs in a tracked file — your project's backlog or
  debt register — with the verdict artifact named. Do not treat the index as the
  durable home for a decision.

### Centralized review logs

Verdict artifacts land under `logs/<backend>/reviews/`. The merge gate writes
each panel child into its own run-owned directory first and CENTRALIZES those
artifacts afterwards — copied atomically (staged beside the destination, swapped
by rename-with-replace, so a reader never sees a partial verdict at its final
name) and only from QUIESCENT sources: a directory whose child could not be
confirmed exited is skipped and rides the preserved run dir instead.

What that means when reading logs:

- **A verdict at its final name is complete.** There is no half-written state to
  guard against, so a filename is a safe handle to cite.
- **A missing verdict is not a lost one.** An unconfirmed child's artifacts stay
  in the preserved run directory, which the merge output names. Look there
  before concluding a review produced nothing.
- **The central directories auto-prune** when `CROSS_REVIEW_PRUNE_TOOL` names
  a safe-delete tool (unset means nothing is deleted) — except while the follow-up index
  carries an unsealed legacy region, which quarantines every prune until the
  operator runs the seal mode. An index nobody clears therefore stops verdict
  retention for the whole repository, which is why the capture lifecycle above
  treats clearing as maintenance with a deadline.

Cite a verdict by FILENAME. It is stable, greppable, and survives the pruning a
line number does not.

### Orphan review children (the foreground cap)

Review and audit children are launched with a bound and are never left running
past it. The contract the gate implements, and the one to preserve in anything
that launches a child here:

- **A timeout terminates the process TREE**, not just the child. A native
  descendant — `git`, `ping`, a spawned tool — outlives a parent-only kill and
  keeps writing into a directory the run is about to clean.
- **Termination is CONFIRMED, not assumed.** The child is waited on after the
  kill; only a confirmed exit clears the flag.
- **An UNCONFIRMED exit preserves the run and trusted directories.** The gate
  would otherwise delete artifacts from under a process still writing them.
  Preserved directories are named in the output for manual reaping — they are
  evidence, not litter.
- **A killed child's output is PARTIAL by construction** and is not parsed for a
  summary. A terminated run reports what it is rather than presenting a
  truncated capture as a result.

The failure this prevents is silent: an orphaned reviewer keeps burning tokens,
writes verdicts nothing consumes, and can leave artifacts that a later run reads
as its own. If a change here cannot confirm termination, it must fail toward
preservation.

### SCOPED capture (opt-in, and dormant until a project opts in)

A reviewer may mark a QUALITY finding `SCOPED: <slug>` when the gap it names is
already registered as deferred work, and the wrappers carry that slug through as
the row's routing tag. The reviewer emits it only after passing the CANONICAL
DECLARATION AUDIT defined in `scripts/codex/review-prompt-template.md` — live
placement and identity, the package field floor plus authority-document
specialization, gap match, authority predating the diff, and separability. The
SLUG is the entire claim — text after it on the marker line is for a human
reading the verdict and never reaches the index. A marker that is not on the
finding's first indented body line, a slug outside the grammar, or any failed
package or project-specific authority rule produces no tag. Every such case
falls back to ordinary uncaptured duty, never to a silent clearance.

Identity means EXACTLY ONE entry across the whole authority set. A slug the set
answers twice resolves to nothing, because the marker carries only the slug and
so cannot say which entry it means — and the entries can differ in gap,
authority, or separability, so picking between them would let a favourable one
clear a row another would refuse. This is why a register forbids reusing a slug:
reuse costs capture rather than sharing it.

**SCOPED capture stays dormant unless the project declares an AUTHORITY SET.**
The project-owned list below names the documents whose entries may authorize a
deferral:

<!-- crg-render-slot v1 scoped-authority BEGIN -->
- No authority documents. SCOPED capture is dormant.
<!-- crg-render-slot v1 scoped-authority END -->

When the list contains the no-authority sentinel, no slug resolves, nothing is
captured, and every row is ordinary repair duty. That is a safe default, not a
broken one.

What capture buys is re-auditability, not clearance: a tagged row records that a
live declaration owns the gap, so a later batch pass RE-RUNS the same numbered
audit against the entry rather than re-deriving the judgement. Only a passing
full audit clears a tagged row; a partial check — gap match alone — can erase
duty for an inseparable, runtime-breaking gap, which is why the audit is
numbered and total.

The package checks are a universal floor, never the complete schema. The field
floor is slug, gap, authority, and separability. After exactly one entry
resolves, apply every stricter validation
and pairing rule declared by the resolved authority document. Such rules
include additional required fields, accepted authority shapes, split-specific
scope, field pairings, and authority-delivery pairings. Project specialization
may only narrow capture. No project rule can weaken a package check, change
finding severity or category, or relax verdict-output requirements.

The verdict file contains a `VERDICT:` line which is one of:
- `VERDICT: CLEAN`
- `VERDICT: NON-BLOCKING`
- `VERDICT: BLOCKED`

The prompt requires a per-category enumeration BEFORE the verdict
line, so the wrapper locates the verdict by the `^VERDICT:` prefix
anywhere in a pass's output (not by line position).

## Why The Isolation Model (do not retry the old approach)

An earlier design ran `codex exec --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`
with cwd = the live worktree and a prompt telling Codex to fetch the diff
via git. This corrupted `.git/worktrees/<wt>/index` on every gated
commit, via two distinct vectors, both now fixed:

1. The review ran in the live repo; Codex (an autonomous agent carrying
   its own plugin-marketplace git scaffolding) spliced a foreign tree
   into the worktree index. → Fixed by the isolated git-less trusted
   scratch root.
2. `git commit` exports `GIT_INDEX_FILE` / `GIT_DIR` into the
   pre-commit hook environment; Codex's plugin subsystem runs its own
   `git` independent of `-C`/sandbox/prompt and inherited those vars,
   writing plugin objects into the worktree index ("Error building
   trees"). → Fixed by the `GIT_*` environment scrub around the codex
   child.

Never reintroduce `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`;
never point Codex's working root at the reviewed tree; never let the
reviewer run git in the live repo; keep the `GIT_*` scrub. Validating
the wrapper by invoking it directly gives FALSE confidence — vector 2
only reproduces under a real `git commit`, so verify gate changes
through an actual normally reviewed commit, not just a direct wrapper run.
